  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nDry surface high pressure will be in place across northeast\nMinnesota the rest of today and into Thursday. A cold front will\nenter northeast Minnesota late Thursday evening and slowly move\neast across northwest Wisconsin through the day on Friday.\nInitially, low level moisture is expected to mix out through the\nafternoon ahead of the front to favor a dry forecast prior to\n00Z. Thereafter, as low level moisture recovers, the low level\njet modestly increases and a series of mid level impulses\npropagate along the International Border south of the main wave,\nscattered rain showers and thunderstorms are expected Thursday\nnight across northeast Minnesota with the passage of the front.\nWith limited buoyancy, severe weather is not expected.\n\nRain shower and thunderstorm chances increase on Friday with\ndaytime heating and bit better buoyancy building as the front\nslowly moves across the area. A few stronger storms with small\nhail and wind gusts over 40 mph are expected across northwest\nWisconsin. However, the overall threat for severe weather is\nlow. \n\nFor the rest of the forecast for this weekend and into next\nweek, near zonal flow is favored across the lower 48. This\noverall large scale flow pattern favors near normal \ntemperatures with high temperatures in the 70s to lower 80s. The\nmain storm track is favored to remain south of the Northland, \nhowever, low predictability impulses embedded in the faster flow\naloft and daytime heating would present a near daily, sporadic \ncoverage of rain showers and thunderstorms (isolated to \nscattered coverage). Overall, with limited buoyancy, severe \nweather is not likely.",
